1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,760
Nocturia - more than half of adults over 55 deal with this.

2
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:06,880
Here are 3 causes beyond an enlarged prostate.

3
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:16,640
Waking up to urinate two or more times a night isn't just a normal part of aging - it's called nocturia, and more than half of adults over 55 deal with it.

4
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:24,800
An enlarged prostate is only one possible cause - evening drinks, medication timing, and how well you sleep all play a role too.

5
00:00:25,120 --> 00:00:27,520
Three things tend to drive this the most.

6
00:00:27,840 --> 00:00:35,200
Alcohol and caffeine in the evening, taking blood pressure or diuretic medication close to bedtime, and untreated sleep apnea.

7
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:38,080
First, alcohol and caffeine in the evening.

8
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:46,560
Both act as diuretics and blunt the hormone that normally concentrates your urine overnight, so more fluid ends up leaving your body while you sleep.

9
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:50,880
Second, taking blood pressure or diuretic medication close to bedtime.

10
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:56,240
It's still working while you sleep, which pushes urine output up right when you don't want it to.

11
00:00:56,560 --> 00:00:59,760
Asking your doctor about an earlier dose can make a real difference.

12
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:02,800
Third, loud snoring or sleep apnea.

13
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:13,920
Each pause in breathing changes the pressure in your chest, which triggers a hormone that ramps up urine production overnight - treating the sleep apnea often cuts down the nighttime bathroom trips too.

14
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:17,840
Cutting alcohol and caffeine two to three hours before bed helps.

15
00:01:18,160 --> 00:01:25,360
Ask your doctor about taking blood pressure or diuretic medication earlier in the day, and if you snore loudly, ask about a sleep study.
